::: {.doc-subject}

Create a **Great Tables** object.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/gt.py#L240-L648){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.CLASS}
```python
GT(
    data,
    rowname_col=None,
    groupname_col=None,
    auto_align=True,
    id=None,
    locale=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

The `GT()` class creates the `GT` object when provided with tabular data. Using this class is
the the first step in a typical **Great Tables** workflow. Once we have this object, we can
take advantage of numerous methods to get the desired display table for publication.

There are a few table structuring options we can consider at this stage. We can choose to create
a table stub containing row labels through the use of the `rowname_col=` argument. Further to
this, row groups can be created with the `groupname_col=` argument. Both arguments take the name
of a column in the input table data. Typically, the data in the `groupname_col=` column will
consist of categorical text whereas the data in the `rowname_col=` column will often contain
unique labels (perhaps being unique across the entire table or unique only within the different
row groups).
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[data]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A DataFrame object.

<code>[rowname_col]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | list[str] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column name in the input `data=` table to use as row labels to be placed in the table
    stub. A list of column names creates a hierarchical stub, where the last column holds the
    row labels and the columns before it are outer levels (outermost first). Repeated values in
    the outer levels are merged across adjacent rows. A merged cell takes its row striping and
    any style (e.g., a `data_color()` fill) from the first of the rows it spans.

<code>[groupname_col]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column name in the input `data=` table to use as group labels for generation of row
    groups.

<code>[auto_align]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optionally have column data be aligned depending on the content contained in each column of
    the input `data=`.

<code>[id]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   By default (with `None`) the table ID will be a random, ten-letter string as generated
    through internal use of the `random_id()` function. A custom table ID can be used here by
    providing a string.

<code>[locale]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   An optional locale identifier that can be set as the default locale for all functions that
    take a `locale` argument. Examples include `"en"` for English (United States) and `"fr"`
    for French (France).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A GT object is returned.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Let's use the `exibble` dataset for the next few examples, we'll learn how to make simple
output tables with the `GT()` class. The most basic thing to do is to just use `GT()` with the
dataset as the input.

::: {#c3b4e160 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT, exibble

GT(exibble)
```

